# Narration for src/content/posts/this-site-1-1.mdx. A header line per segment, then its SSML on one line.
# "@ 012 p gap=1200" overrides the silence before a segment, in milliseconds.
# node scripts/narration/narrate.mjs check posts/this-site-1-1
voice en-US-BrianMultilingualNeural
lang en-IE
pitch -1.5%
recorded 2026-10-24

@ 001 intro
Welcome to Tim writes about dev.<break time="400ms"/> I'm Brian, an AI voice,<break time="150ms"/> and Tim has asked me to read you the post,<break time="200ms"/> This site,<break time="150ms"/> 1.1.<break time="500ms"/> I skip the code and the footnotes, so for those,<break time="150ms"/> check the post.

@ 002 p
In an endless sea of app updates, it wouldn't be right if I didn't announce version 1.1 of this site within a month of launch.<break time="350ms"/> How would the investors know we're doing cool things?

@ 003 p
In software engineering we like to manufacture false drama for the marketing team.<break time="300ms"/> That usually means a post-release content patch, or a whole set of features we realised were important but didn't release,<break time="200ms"/> because of reasons <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">usually resourcing</prosody>.<break time="350ms"/> The truth is that most of the time we set the criteria for an <sub alias="Minimum viable product, M V P,">MVP</sub>, deliver that as the first release or a major one, and then follow it up with at least a <sub alias="point one">.1</sub> release,<break time="150ms"/> carrying the features and fixes that didn't make the <sub alias="M V P">MVP</sub> sprint.

@ 004 p
This post celebrates that tradition.<break time="300ms"/> I decided on the <sub alias="M V P">MVP</sub> that launched the site and immediately started a list of things to change.<break time="250ms"/> I wasn't alone in it, either:<break time="200ms"/> the team behind this website had feedback of its own.<break time="300ms"/> I guess it's time to meet the team, because nothing in modern agentic AI workflows is a one-person effort any more.

@ 005 p
But first,<break time="150ms"/> a classic list of release notes for your reading <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">or listening</prosody> pleasure.

@ 006 h
Release notes:<break time="250ms"/> 1.1,<break time="200ms"/> already.

@ 007 p
This release of <sub alias="Tim writes about dev">Tim writes [about] dev</sub> includes many things that will excite<break time="100ms"/> and<break time="100ms"/> <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ɔː">awe</phoneme> you:

@ 008 h
Improving your experience,<break time="150ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">with features no one asked for yet</prosody>.

@ 009 li
Most pictures can now go full screen.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">wow, pixels!</prosody>

@ 010 li
Tables can now go full screen on mobile.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">much data,<break time="150ms"/> very interesting</prosody>.

@ 011 li
Generative AI images now carry a burnt-in label,<break time="150ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">in the spirit of the <sub alias="E U">EU</sub> AI Act</prosody>.

@ 012 li
The posts page now has a jump list for years and months.

@ 013 li
A short version up top, for the <sub alias="Too long, didn’t read, T L D R,">TL;DR</sub> crowd,<break time="150ms"/> and anyone who needs a refresher or a hook to read on.

@ 014 li
Basic diagrams can now be used in posts.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">there's one in Meet the cast</prosody>.

@ 015 li
Posts and pages narrated in full,<break time="150ms"/> by Brian.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">more on him below</prosody>.

@ 016 li
The logo is now the identity matrix.

@ 017 li
Mathematical symbols drift in the margins,<break time="150ms"/> for the wide-screen readers.

@ 018 li
Posts were turned into cards.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">because cards are better for content discoverability!</prosody>

@ 019 h
Squashing of bugs.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">they were <prosody rate="-4%" pitch="+0.75%">features</prosody> once</prosody>.

@ 020 li
Wide tables were cropped on mobile browsers.

@ 021 li
The <sub alias="Really Simple Syndication, R S S,">RSS</sub> feed and the sitemap showed as a wall of text,<break time="150ms"/> instead of something pretty.

@ 022 li
Images broke in Edge's Immersive Reader.

@ 023 li
Pesky em dashes were swapped out,<break time="200ms"/> because apparently they're too generative AI for a site about agentic AI.

@ 024 li
The home page was made less boring.

@ 025 li
About was made less boring.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">read all the things</prosody>.

@ 026 h
Things we kept the same,<break time="200ms"/> but they seem new,<break time="100ms"/> so they're in anyway.

@ 027 li
No analytics,<break time="150ms"/> and no third-party anything.

@ 028 li
No special scripts for the narration audio,<break time="200ms"/> just smart serving of static content and modern browsers.

@ 029 h
Meet the cast.

@ 030 p
What you've all been waiting for:<break time="250ms"/> an exclusive look behind the scenes of this parasocial relationship,<break time="200ms"/> and the cast that makes this site possible:

@ 031 caption
Picture caption.<break time="300ms"/> Meet the cast,<break time="150ms"/> Brady Bunch style:<break time="250ms"/> Tim,<break time="250ms"/> <lang xml:lang="ja-JP">相棒オプスさん</lang>,<break time="100ms"/> the Collaborator Opus,<break time="250ms"/> <lang xml:lang="ja-JP">開発オプスさん</lang>,<break time="100ms"/> the Developer Opus,<break time="250ms"/> <lang xml:lang="ja-JP">校正オプスさん</lang>,<break time="100ms"/> the Proofreader Opus,<break time="250ms"/> Ms Claudie Mittens,<break time="250ms"/> <lang xml:lang="ja-JP">読者オプスさん</lang>,<break time="100ms"/> the Reader Opus,<break time="250ms"/> <lang xml:lang="ja-JP">研究者オプスさん</lang>,<break time="100ms"/> the Researcher Opus,<break time="250ms"/> ye, the readers,<break time="250ms"/> and everyone's favourite professional AI voice,<break time="200ms"/> Brian.

@ 032 li
Tim.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">this is me obviously, but it's a version I construct for our flourishing parasocial relationship!<break time="150ms"/> Yay!</prosody><break time="300ms"/> A mysterious figure, only ever seen from behind <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">or blacked out</prosody>, never in portrait,<break time="150ms"/> who spends countless hours developing the content by typing vigorously on a keyboard.<break time="300ms"/> A holder of <lang xml:lang="it-IT">Magister Scientiae</lang> <sub alias=", a Master of Science, in">in</sub> Artificial Intelligence.

@ 033 li
Opus.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">Anthropic's Claude model</prosody>.<break time="300ms"/> Opus is a jack of all trades,<break time="150ms"/> and only <prosody rate="-4%" pitch="+0.75%">great</prosody> at them.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">not a <prosody rate="-4%" pitch="+0.75%">master</prosody>,<break time="100ms"/> that would be a</prosody> <lang xml:lang="it-IT">magister</lang> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">in Latin</prosody>.

@ 034 li
Collaborator.<break time="300ms"/> acts as a sounding board for post ideas, and turns after-work-brain Tim into consumable content.<break time="150ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">yum!</prosody>

@ 035 li
Developer.<break time="300ms"/> happily writes all the code that keeps the site serving content,<break time="100ms"/> and our parasocial relationship alive.

@ 036 li
Proofreader.<break time="300ms"/> basically a secretary,<break time="150ms"/> whose job is to turn my poor grammar, spelling mistakes and occasional awkward prose <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">because who doesn't write slower than they think?</prosody><break time="150ms"/> into readable human thought.<break time="300ms"/> Also makes sure I don't sound like I have multiple voices.<break time="150ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">brand consistency!</prosody>

@ 037 li
Reader.<break time="300ms"/> an<break time="250ms"/> <prosody volume="-20%" rate="+4%">unwilling</prosody><break time="450ms"/> instance of Opus <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">a sub-agent, for those familiar with the term</prosody><break time="150ms"/> that reviews the page as if it was one of ye.<break time="300ms"/> It usually complains about my jokes,<break time="150ms"/> and its feedback I<break time="250ms"/> <prosody volume="-20%" rate="+4%">always</prosody><break time="500ms"/> sometimes ignore.

@ 038 li
Researcher.<break time="300ms"/> as I lack a photographic memory,<break time="150ms"/> Opus will go and fact-check anything that's<break time="150ms"/> <sub alias="suss">sus</sub>,<break time="200ms"/> or that ye might doubt.

@ 039 li
Ms Claudie Mittens:<break time="200ms"/> a graceful hypothetical cat,<break time="100ms"/> once a kitten,<break time="150ms"/> who has eaten all the cookies on the site,<break time="100ms"/> and happily walks across the keyboard as part of her classical music training.<break time="300ms"/> She's the team's mascot, named after Claude,<break time="150ms"/> and Mittens because it rhymes with Claudie.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">I don't know how rhyme</prosody>.

@ 040 li
Brian, an AI voice:<break time="200ms"/> our multilingual expert neural voice.<break time="300ms"/> Brian speaks 92 languages and accents,<break time="150ms"/> and Microsoft bills him as a sincere, calm and approachable young man,<break time="100ms"/> made for podcasts and chat.<break time="350ms"/> But to us here on <sub alias="Tim writes about dev">Tim writes [about] dev</sub>,<break time="150ms"/> he's just Brian,<break time="100ms"/> an AI voice.<break time="300ms"/> He narrates everything here,<break time="150ms"/> and every day is a new day for him,<break time="150ms"/> since Brian has no texturiser.

@ 041 diagram gap=1100
Diagram.<break time="250ms"/> How a post gets made.

@ 042 step
Draft,<break time="100ms"/> by Tim.<break time="250ms"/> Types vigorously,<break time="100ms"/> after work and at weekends.

@ 043 step
Find the gaps,<break time="100ms"/> by Opus.<break time="250ms"/> Brainstorms, asks what's missing, checks the facts.<break time="250ms"/> Repeated until it holds up.

@ 044 step
Copy pass,<break time="100ms"/> by Opus.<break time="250ms"/> Tidies the grammar,<break time="100ms"/> keeps the voice.

@ 045 step
Cold read,<break time="100ms"/> by Opus.<break time="250ms"/> Reads it as one of ye.<break time="250ms"/> Complains about the jokes.

@ 046 step
Read aloud,<break time="100ms"/> by Brian.<break time="250ms"/> First day on the job,<break time="150ms"/> every time.

@ 047 caption gap=900
Diagram caption.<break time="300ms"/> How a post gets from my head to your eyes <sub alias="and, or">and/or</sub> ears.<break time="700ms"/> <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ɔːɹ">Or</phoneme> I guess,<break time="100ms"/> your tokens and weights if you’re an AI agent?

@ 048 h
What's next.

@ 049 p
Like all good services,<break time="150ms"/> here's a hopeful list of things that will probably happen at some vague,<break time="100ms"/> unspecified time in the future:

@ 050 li
A timeline for the projects page.<break time="150ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">scrolling</prosody>.

@ 051 li
A fancy search for posts and projects,<break time="150ms"/> all powered by JavaScript.<break time="200ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">sorry Opus, that'll be another script!</prosody>

@ 052 li
Support for more kinds of media in posts,<break time="100ms"/> like video.

@ 053 li
More AI voices,<break time="200ms"/> perhaps a Peking opera narration,<break time="100ms"/> for extra drama?

@ 054 p gap=1500
Just like your favourite TV show from the 1950s,<break time="150ms"/> <prosody pitch="-0.75%" rate="+4%">not the Bradys, they're far too modern</prosody>,<break time="250ms"/> we'll go on releasing new versions,<break time="150ms"/> adding background characters,<break time="100ms"/> and setting off on non-canon adventures,<break time="250ms"/> all while keeping both our readers and our investors happy!

@ 055 outro
This post was last updated on 24 October 2026.<break time="300ms"/> This recording was made on 24 October 2026 for <sub alias="Tim Writes dot dev">timwrites.dev</sub>.

@ 056 egg
My favourite TV show?<break time="400ms"/> A chat show,<break time="150ms"/> obviously.<break time="500ms"/> Coming soon,<break time="200ms"/> a podcast:<break time="300ms"/> Brian talks about<break time="100ms"/> Tim writes about dev.<break time="500ms"/> <prosody rate="-4%" volume="-8%">Opus wants five episodes.</prosody><break time="1000ms"/> Right so.<break time="400ms"/> Mind yourselves.
